  "hints": [
    "Overlap checks against every other range are O(n²). What ordering makes overlapping ranges neighbours?",
    "Sort by start. Then a range can only overlap the last merged range.",
    "If start <= last.end, extend last.end to max(last.end, end); otherwise start a new merged range."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function mergeRanges(ranges) {\n  const sorted = ranges.map(([start, end]) => [start, end]).sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0])\n  const merged = []\n  for (const [start, end] of sorted) {\n    const last = merged[merged.length - 1]\n    if (last && start <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end)\n    else merged.push([start, end])\n  }\n  return merged\n}\n",
    "py": "def merge_ranges(ranges):\n    merged = []\n    for start, end in sorted(ranges):\n        if merged and start <= merged[-1][1]:\n            merged[-1][1] = max(merged[-1][1], end)\n        else:\n            merged.append([start, end])\n    return merged\n",
    "explanation": "After sorting by start, every range that could overlap the current merged block starts inside it. One sweep either extends the last block or opens a new one. Taking max(end) handles ranges nested inside earlier ones."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n log n)",
    "space": "O(n)",
    "notes": "Sorting dominates. Copying pairs before sorting keeps the caller's input unchanged."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Insert one new range into an already merged, sorted list in O(n) without re-sorting."
  ]
